# 5. SquashFS Layer Investigation

## 5.1 Main filesystem layer

The first image is:

```text
minimal.squashfs
```

It contains the bulk of the operating system filesystem.

Inspection revealed standard Linux filesystem directories, including:

- `/bin`
- `/boot`
- `/dev`
- `/etc`
- `/usr`

The extracted filesystem includes kernel files for version `7.0.0-30-generic`.

This differs from the running development VM's kernel version. That is expected because the ISO contains its own live operating system.

## 5.2 Standard filesystem layer

The second image is:

```text
minimal.standard.squashfs
```

Its inspected contents include additional system configuration and application-related files.

Examples include:

- AppArmor configuration.
- APT configuration.
- Alternatives configuration.

Its package manifest did not list the Calamares packages found in the live-specific layer.

## 5.3 Live filesystem layer

The third image is:

```text
minimal.standard.live.squashfs
```

This image is approximately 27.2 MiB.

Its inspected contents include:

```text
/etc/calamares/branding/lubuntu/
```

It also contains:

- `branding.desc`
- Calamares module configuration files.
- Lubuntu-specific installer branding assets.

Its package manifest includes:

```text
calamares
calamares-data
calamares-settings-lubuntu
calamares-settings-ubuntu-common
calamares-settings-ubuntu-common-data
casper
libcalamares3.3
libcalamaresui3.3
```

The Calamares package version recorded in the manifest is:

```text
3.3.14-0ubuntu25
```

This establishes that the live-specific layer contains Calamares packages and installer configuration.

It does not, by itself, establish that every installer dependency resides exclusively in this image. The final assembled filesystem must be considered when determining actual runtime availability.

