# 7. Casper Boot Investigation

Casper is responsible for locating and assembling the live filesystem during boot.

The investigation has identified several important components.

## 7.1 Default layer selection

The extracted configuration file is:

```text
conf/conf.d/default-layer.conf
```

Its contents are:

```bash
LAYERFS_PATH=minimal.standard.live.squashfs
```

This establishes the default selected layer.

## 7.2 Kernel command-line override

The Casper script contains:

```bash
layerfs-path=*)
    export LAYERFS_PATH="${x#layerfs-path=}"
```

This allows the selected layer to be overridden through a kernel command-line argument.

The extracted GRUB configuration does not specify this argument, so the default configuration applies to the normal boot entry.

## 7.3 Layer dependency discovery

Casper derives the parent layer names by repeatedly removing the final dotted component from the selected filename.

Starting with:

```text
minimal.standard.live.squashfs
```

It derives:

```text
minimal.standard.squashfs
minimal.squashfs
```

The script checks that the required images exist before continuing.

The resulting layer chain is:

```text
minimal.squashfs
        |
        v
minimal.standard.squashfs
        |
        v
minimal.standard.live.squashfs
```

This is a chained filesystem layout, not three unrelated images.

## 7.4 Read-only mounting

Casper obtains a backing device for each image, identifies its filesystem type, and mounts it read-only.

The script uses:

```bash
mount -t "${fstype}" -o ro,noatime \
  "${backdev}" "${croot}${imagename}"
```

The individual layer mount points are collected into the variables:

```text
rofsstring
rofslist
```

These variables are subsequently used during filesystem assembly.

