# 12. Implications for OxideOS Development

The investigation has identified several architectural requirements.

## 12.1 Preserve the original boot architecture

The initial OxideOS build should preserve:

- The original GRUB configuration and boot entries.
- The original kernel and initramfs.
- The SquashFS layer filenames.
- The SquashFS dependency chain.
- Casper configuration and scripts.
- OverlayFS functionality.
- Calamares packages and configuration.

Changing any of these before establishing a successful baseline introduces unnecessary risk.

## 12.2 Installer dependencies must be considered across all layers

The live-specific layer contains Calamares packages and configuration.

However, the complete installer runtime depends on the assembled filesystem.

We must not assume that the small live layer independently contains everything required by the installer.

The installed system and live environment must also be treated as separate execution environments.

## 12.3 Build tooling must preserve ISO bootability

The next build-tool investigation concerns `livefs-editor`.

The project must determine whether it can correctly process this exact Lubuntu desktop ISO.

The upstream tool's primary focus is Ubuntu Server installer media. Desktop ISO compatibility has not been sufficiently established for our use case.

This is a technical uncertainty, not yet an evidenced blocker.

No tool switch should be made until testing provides a reason.

