# 15. Conclusion

The OxideOS project has progressed from examining the Lubuntu ISO as a packaged distribution to understanding significant portions of its underlying live boot architecture.

The most important discovery is the chained SquashFS and OverlayFS design.

Lubuntu does not simply boot a single compressed filesystem. Its initramfs uses Casper to locate the installation media, select the appropriate filesystem layers, mount those layers read-only, and combine them into a writable live environment.

The investigation has also confirmed that the live-specific layer contains Calamares packages and Lubuntu-specific installer configuration.

These findings provide a technical foundation for the next stage of OxideOS development.

However, the investigation is not complete. The final initramfs handoff still needs to be examined, and the compatibility of `livefs-editor` with the Lubuntu desktop ISO must be tested.

The project should continue to prioritize understanding and preserving the existing boot architecture before introducing customizations.

**The next milestone is not a prettier ISO or a smaller ISO. It is a working, installable, repeatable OxideOS baseline.**

